\label{ch:ssa}

Static Single Assignment (SSA) form is the dominant intermediate representation in modern compilers. In SSA form, every variable is assigned exactly once, and phi functions merge values at control-flow join points. This simple constraint makes data dependencies explicit, enabling a wide range of optimizations to be expressed as local transformations. This chapter develops the theory and practice of SSA, from construction algorithms to out-of-SSA translation.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 56.1
%% ============================================================
\section{SSA: Definition and Properties}\label{sec:ssa-definition}

\begin{definition}[SSA Form]\label{def:ssa}
A program is in \emph{Static Single Assignment} form if every variable is defined exactly once. That is, for every variable $v$, there is exactly one assignment $v = \ldots$ in the program.
\end{definition}

At control-flow join points where different definitions of a variable may reach, \emph{phi functions} select the appropriate definition:

\begin{verbatim}
  B1: x1 = 1
      if (cond) goto B2 else goto B3
  B2: x2 = 2
      goto B4
  B3: x3 = 3
      goto B4
  B4: x4 = phi(x2, x3)  // x4 = x2 if from B2, x3 if from B3
      y = x4 + 1
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Properties of SSA}\label{sec:ssa-properties}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Single definition.} Each variable has exactly one defining assignment.
  \item \textbf{Explicit data flow.} The use of a variable directly references its definition.
  \item \textbf{Def-use chains.} The def-use chains are explicit in the IR (no dataflow analysis needed).
  \item \textbf{Dominance.} A variable's definition dominates all its uses.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Dominance Property]\label{thm:ssa-dominance}
In SSA form, the definition of every variable $v$ dominates all uses of $v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $v$ is defined exactly once, and the definition must be reached before any use, the definition dominates all uses. \qed
\end{proof}

