\section{Construction Algorithms}\label{sec:ssa-construction}

Converting a program to SSA form involves two steps: inserting phi functions and renaming variables.

\subsection{Cytron et al. Algorithm}\label{sec:cytron-algorithm}

The classic algorithm by Cytron et al.~\cite{cytron1991} uses dominance frontiers to place phi functions.

\subsubsection{Step 1: Phi Function Placement}

For each variable $v$ defined in block $B$, insert phi functions at all blocks in the \emph{iterated dominance frontier} of $B$.

\begin{definition}[Iterated Dominance Frontier]\label{def:idf}
The \emph{iterated dominance frontier} of a set of blocks $S$, written $\mathrm{IDF}(S)$, is the least fixed point of:
\[
  \mathrm{IDF}(S) = \mathrm{DF}(S) \cup \mathrm{DF}(\mathrm{IDF}(S))
\]
where $\mathrm{DF}(S) = \bigcup_{D \in S} \mathrm{DF}(D)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Phi Function Placement (Cytron et al.)}\label{alg:phi-placement}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{PlacePhiFunctions}{$\mathrm{CFG}$}
  \For{each variable $v$}
    \Let{\mathrm{defs}(v)}{\text{set of blocks where } v \text{ is defined}}
    \Let{\mathrm{phi\_blocks}}{\mathrm{IDF}(\mathrm{defs}(v))}
    \For{each block $B \in \mathrm{phi\_blocks}$}
      \State Insert $v \gets \phi(v, v, \ldots)$ at the beginning of $B$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{Step 2: Variable Renaming}

After phi function placement, rename variables so that each definition is unique:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{SSA Renaming (Cytron et al.)}\label{alg:ssa-rename}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Rename}{$B, \mathrm{stack}$}
  \For{each instruction $v = \ldots$ in $B$}
    \State $i \gets \mathrm{stack}[v].\mathrm{push\_new}()$
    \State Rename $v$ to $v_i$
  \EndFor
  \For{each use of $v$ in $B$}
    \State Replace with $\mathrm{stack}[v].\mathrm{top}()$
  \EndFor
  \For{each successor $S$ of $B$}
    \For{each phi function $v_j \gets \phi(\ldots)$ in $S$}
      \State Set the argument corresponding to $B$ to $\mathrm{stack}[v].\mathrm{top}()$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
  \For{each child $C$ of $B$ in the dominator tree}
    \State $\mathrm{Rename}(C, \mathrm{stack})$
  \EndFor
  \For{each definition $v_i$ in $B$}
    \State $\mathrm{stack}[v].\mathrm{pop}()$
  \EndFor
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Brauner et al. Algorithm}\label{sec:brauner-algorithm}

The algorithm by Brauner et al.~\cite{brauner2013} simplifies SSA construction by using a different strategy for phi function placement. Instead of computing dominance frontiers, it traverses the dominator tree and inserts phi functions based on the live range of each variable.

\subsection{Minimal SSA}\label{sec:minimal-ssa}

The Cytron algorithm produces \emph{minimal} SSA form: it inserts the minimum number of phi functions needed to ensure the single-assignment property. Some phi functions may be \emph{redundant} (all arguments are the same variable); these can be removed in a cleanup pass.

