\section{Phi Functions and Their Semantics}\label{sec:phi-semantics}

Phi functions are not real operations; they are a notational device for merging values at control-flow join points.

\subsection{Semantics}\label{sec:phi-sem}

The semantics of a phi function $v \gets \phi(v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n)$ is:
\[
  v = \begin{cases}
    v_1 & \text{if control came from predecessor 1} \\
    v_2 & \text{if control came from predecessor 2} \\
    \vdots \\
    v_n & \text{if control came from predecessor } n
  \end{cases}
\]

Phi functions execute \emph{in parallel}: all phi functions in a block read their arguments before any of them write their results.

\subsection{Phi Functions as Parallel Assignment}\label{sec:phi-parallel}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before (non-SSA):
  B1: x = 1; goto B3
  B2: x = 2; goto B3
  B3: y = x + 1

  // After (SSA):
  B1: x1 = 1; goto B3
  B2: x2 = 2; goto B3
  B3: x3 = phi(x1, x2); y = x3 + 1
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Phi Function Optimization}\label{sec:phi-optimization}

Several optimizations can simplify phi functions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Phi elimination.} If all arguments are the same variable, the phi function is redundant: $v \gets \phi(x, x) \implies v = x$.
  \item \textbf{Copy propagation.} If $v \gets \phi(x, y)$ and $x = y$ at the join point, the phi function is redundant.
  \item \textbf{Phi forwarding.} If a phi function's result is used only in one successor, it can be replaced by a simple copy.
\end{itemize}

