\section{SSA for Memory (Memory SSA)}\label{sec:memory-ssa}

Standard SSA applies to scalar variables. Extending it to memory (arrays, objects, pointers) requires additional machinery.

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:memory-ssa-problem}

Memory operations (loads and stores) can alias: a store through one pointer may affect a load through another pointer. This creates implicit data dependencies that are not captured by standard SSA.

\subsection{Memory SSA}\label{sec:memory-ssa-form}

Memory SSA extends SSA by treating the \emph{memory state} as a virtual variable:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before:
  *p = 1;
  x = *q;  // may or may not read the value written through p

  // After (Memory SSA):
  mem1 = store(mem0, p, 1);
  x = load(mem1, q);  // explicit memory dependency
\end{verbatim}

Each memory operation takes the current memory state as input and produces a new memory state. Phi functions merge memory states at join points.

\subsection{Alias Analysis for Memory SSA}\label{sec:alias-memory-ssa}

The effectiveness of Memory SSA depends on alias analysis: if the compiler can prove that two pointers do not alias, it can avoid creating unnecessary memory phi functions.

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:memory-ssa-limits}

Memory SSA can be imprecise when alias information is incomplete. Conservative alias analysis may create phi functions for memory states that are not actually needed, leading to pessimistic optimization.

