\section{Out-of-SSA Translation}\label{sec:out-of-ssa}

Before code generation, SSA form must be converted back to a non-SSA form (since real machines do not have phi functions).

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:out-of-ssa-problem}

Phi functions must be replaced by copy instructions that move the appropriate value into the phi function's result variable:

\begin{verbatim}
  // SSA:
  B1: x1 = 1; goto B3
  B2: x2 = 2; goto B3
  B3: x3 = phi(x1, x2)

  // Out of SSA:
  B1: x = 1; goto B3
  B2: x = 2; goto B3
  B3: // x already has the right value (no copy needed!)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Coalescing}\label{sec:coalescing}

The key optimization in out-of-SSA translation is \emph{coalescing}: assigning the phi function's result and its arguments to the same register, eliminating the need for copy instructions.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{SSA Coalescing}\label{alg:coalescing}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Coalesce}{$\mathrm{SSA}$}
  \For{each phi function $v \gets \phi(v_1, \ldots, v_n)$}
    \For{each argument $v_i$ from predecessor $B_i$}
      \If{$v$ and $v_i$ do not interfere}
        \State Assign $v$ and $v_i$ to the same register
      \Else
        \State Insert copy: $v \gets v_i$ at the end of $B_i$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Briggs et al. Algorithm}\label{sec:briggs-algorithm}

The algorithm by Briggs et al.~\cite{briggs1998} integrates coalescing with register allocation, producing efficient code without excessive copy instructions.

\begin{historical}
SSA form was introduced by Rosen et al.~\cite{rosen1988} and popularized by Cytron et al.~\cite{cytron1991}, who gave the first efficient construction algorithm. The connection between SSA and dominance frontiers was a key insight of the Cytron paper. The out-of-SSA translation problem was studied by Briggs et al.~\cite{briggs1998}, who showed that coalescing can eliminate most copy instructions. Memory SSA, developed by several researchers in the 2000s, extends SSA to handle the complexities of memory aliasing. Today, SSA form is the standard IR in virtually all production compilers: LLVM, GCC, HotSpot, V8, and Rust's MIR all use SSA.
\end{historical}

