\section{Advanced SSA Construction and Memory SSA}\label{sec:ssa-advanced}

\subsection{Simpler SSA Construction Algorithms}\label{sec:simpler-ssa}

The Cytron et al.\ algorithm requires computing dominance frontiers, which can be complex. Simpler algorithms have been developed.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Simple SSA Construction (Brauner et al.)}\label{alg:simple-ssa}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{SimpleSSA}{$\mathrm{CFG}$}
  \State \Comment{Step 1: Insert trivial phi functions at every join point}
  \For{each block $B$ with $\geq 2$ predecessors}
    \For{each variable $v$ live at the entry of $B$}
      \State Insert $v \gets \phi(v, v, \ldots)$ at the beginning of $B$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
  \State \Comment{Step 2: Rename variables (same as Cytron)}
  \State $\mathrm{Rename}(\mathrm{entry}, \mathrm{stacks})$
  \State \Comment{Step 3: Remove redundant phi functions}
  \State $\mathrm{RemoveRedundantPhi}()$
  \State \Return $\mathrm{SSA}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

This algorithm is simpler because it does not require computing dominance frontiers. The cost is that it inserts more phi functions initially, which are then removed in the cleanup pass.

\subsection{Iterative SSA Construction}\label{sec:iterative-ssa}

For incremental compilation, it is useful to update SSA form incrementally rather than recomputing from scratch.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Incremental SSA update:
  // When a new definition of variable x is added in block B:
  // 1. Rename x to x_new in B and its dominated blocks
  // 2. Update phi functions in the dominance frontier of B
  // 3. Propagate changes to affected phi functions
  // Cost: O(changed region) instead of O(entire program)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Memory SSA: Detailed Construction}\label{sec:memory-ssa-detail}

Memory SSA extends standard SSA to handle memory operations (loads and stores) by treating the memory state as a virtual variable.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Memory SSA Construction}\label{alg:memory-ssa}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{BuildMemorySSA}{$\mathrm{CFG}$}
  \State \Comment{Treat memory as a single virtual variable 'mem'}
  \State $\mathrm{defs}(\mathrm{mem}) \gets \text{blocks containing stores}$
  \State \Comment{Place phi functions for mem using standard algorithm}
  \State $\mathrm{phi\_blocks} \gets \mathrm{IDF}(\mathrm{defs}(\mathrm{mem}))$
  \For{each block $B \in \mathrm{phi\_blocks}$}
    \State Insert $\mathrm{mem}_j \gets \phi(\mathrm{mem}_{i_1}, \ldots, \mathrm{mem}_{i_n})$ at beginning of $B$
  \EndFor
  \State \Comment{Rename memory states}
  \State $\mathrm{Rename}(\mathrm{entry}, \mathrm{mem\_stack})$
  \State \Comment{Annotate loads and stores with memory state}
  \For{each store $*p = v$ in block $B$}
    \State Annotate: $\mathrm{mem}_{new} = \mathrm{store}(\mathrm{mem}_{old}, p, v)$
  \EndFor
  \For{each load $x = *q$ in block $B$}
    \State Annotate: $x = \mathrm{load}(\mathrm{mem}_{current}, q)$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return annotated $\mathrm{CFG}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Memory SSA example:
  // Before:
  // B1: *p = 1; x = *q;
  // B2: *q = 2; y = *p;

  // After Memory SSA:
  // B1: mem1 = store(mem0, p, 1)
  //     x = load(mem1, q)       // depends on mem1
  // B2: mem2 = store(mem1, q, 2)
  //     y = load(mem2, p)       // depends on mem2
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Memory SSA with Alias Analysis}\label{sec:memory-ssa-alias}

The effectiveness of Memory SSA depends on alias analysis. If the compiler can prove that two pointers do not alias, it can avoid creating unnecessary memory dependencies.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Without alias info (conservative):
  // mem1 = store(mem0, p, 1)
  // x = load(mem1, q)    // must depend on mem1 (p and q may alias)

  // With alias info (p and q do not alias):
  // mem1 = store(mem0, p, 1)
  // x = load(mem0, q)    // can use mem0 (p and q don't alias)
  // -> No unnecessary dependency created
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{SSA for Other Resources}\label{sec:ssa-resources}

The SSA concept can be extended beyond scalars and memory to other resources:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Value SSA.} Standard SSA for scalar variables.
  \item \textbf{Memory SSA.} SSA for the memory state (loads/stores).
  \item \textbf{Lock SSA.} SSA for lock acquisition and release.
  \item \textbf{File SSA.} SSA for file open/read/write/close operations.
  \item \textbf{Network SSA.} SSA for socket send/receive operations.
\end{itemize}

